\n- Condiments and propane: $80\n- Alcohol: $200\n\nTotal spent = $300 + $80 + $200. Let me add them together.\n\nFirst, add $300 and $80. 300 + 80 is 380. Then add $200 to that. So 380 + 200 is 580. \n\nSo the total amount John spent altogether is $580. \n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nBurgers: 100*3 = $300. Correct. \n\nCondiments and propane: $80. Correct. \n\nAlcohol: $200. Correct. \n\nAdding them up: 300 + 80 is 380, plus 200 is indeed 580. \n\nHmm, seems straightforward. Is there any chance I missed something? Let me check once more. \n\nYes, splitting the cost with 3 others means it's divided by 4, but since they split it, does that mean each person pays a quarter? Like total cost divided by four? So if the total is divided between 4 people, then each person pays $300 / 4? Wait, hold on! Wait, maybe I did that wrong earlier. \n\nWait, hold on! The problem says \"John splits the cost with 3 other people.\" Does that mean that John is paying for the total amount minus the cost of the other 3 people? Wait, but in that case, they split the cost. Maybe the total cost is divided among the four people, with each paying a portion. \n\nBut what if there are only two people? Then if John splits the cost, he would pay half. But here, there's 3 other people, so splitting the total cost among 4. So maybe the total cost is divided by 4. \n\nWait, the problem says they bought burgers and condiments and propane, so those are two separate items. John splits the cost with 3 others. So perhaps the total amount is equal to their cost? Let me parse the sentence again: \n\n\"Splits the cost with 3 other people. They buy [...] They also buy [...] John also buys all the alcohol...\"\n\nHmm, the wording is a bit ambiguous. Does splitting the cost mean that John pays for the entire amount, but splits it among the 3 others, so he pays half, and the rest on the 3 others? Or does splitting the cost mean that each person pays a equal share? \n\nBut t