bership. So perhaps the sign-up fee is $4 and the membership duration is 4 months. So total cost for that gym would be $30 per month, plus $8? Wait, no, if the sign-up fee is $4, then maybe the total cost is monthly $30 plus $4? So total $34 per month? Which would make sense. So then the total for the first year would be 12 months times $15 total? Wait, no, if the sign-up fee is $50, why is that? Wait, the problem says the first gym is $10/month with a sign-up fee of $50. The second is 3 times more expensive... so $30/month with a sign-up fee of 4 months. So perhaps he pays $50 signup fee to the first gym, and pays $30/month to the second, plus $8? Wait, but he can't pay both. If he buys both, then total would be $50 + $30 + $? If he just buys one, the sign-up fee is separate. Wait, maybe the sign-up fee for both is $50 and $4? But the problem says he gets two different memberships. Maybe it's separate. So total cost would be:\n\nFor the first gym: monthly $10 + $50 = $60 in the first year.\n\nFor the second gym: monthly $30 + sign-up fee of $4. If the sign-up fee is for the 4-month period, then maybe he has to pay $4 as the sign-up fee, and then $30 for the entire 4 months. So total would be $4 + $30 = $34 per month? Then total for the first year would be 12 months * $15?\n\nWait, confusion arises. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal problem:\n\nJohn has two gym memberships. The first is $10 a month with a sign-up fee of $50. The second is 3 times more expensive. The second has a sign-up fee of 4 months.\n\nSo, \"sign-up fee of 4 months membership\". Maybe that means when he signs up for the second gym, he pays $4 as the sign-up fee, and the 4-month membership is for that fee. Therefore, then for the second gym, the monthly cost is $30, plus $4 as a sign-up fee, so total $34 per month. For the first gym, it's $10/month plus $50 sign-up = $60. So total cost for the first year would be 12 months * $60? Wait, no, 12 months times $60 would be $720. 12 months times $60? Tha