2026-10-30 04:07:54 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 20, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint in total. This week, he paints 12 of them. Then next week, he paints 1/4 of the number he did this week. I need to figure out how many ceilings will be left after next week. Hmm.\n\nFirst, let me confirm the numbers. Total ceilings are 28. He paints 12 this week, so that's 12 ceilings. Then next week, if it's 1/4 of the previous week's 12, right? So 1/4 of 12. Let me calculate that. 12 divided by 4 is 3. So he will paint 3 more ceilings next week. Now, the total number of ceilings painted so far would be 12 plus 3, which is 15. Then the remaining ceilings would be 28 minus 15, which is 13. \n\nWait, let me make sure I didn't mix up the fractions. The problem says next week he paints 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. So this week he painted 12, so next week he paints 1/4 of 12, which is indeed 3. That seems right. So total painted is 12 + 3 = 15. 28 minus 15 is 13. So the answer should be 13.\n\nBut let me check again. Total is 28. Painted this week: 12. Painted next week: 1/4 of 12. So 1/4 of 12. 12 divided by 4 is 3. So 12 + 3 is 15. 28 - 15 is indeed 13. Yeah, that seems correct. I don't think I made any mistakes here. Maybe visualize it. If he paints 15 ceilings, there's 13 left. Yeah, that makes sense. \n\nI think that's it. The answer should be 13, which I put in a box.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{13}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, let's break it down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Total Ceilings**\nMichelangelo has a total of **28 ceilings** to paint.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Painted Ceilings This Week**\nHe paints **12 of the ceilings** this week.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Ceilings Painted Next Week**\nThe problem states that next week, he will paint **1/4 the number of ceilings** he painted this week.\n\n$$\n\\text{Ceilings painted next week} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 12 = 3\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Total Ceilings Painted**\nSo far, he has painted:\n\n$$\n12 \\, (\\text{this week}) + 3 \\, (\\text{next week}) = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 5: Ceilings Left to Paint**\nFinally, the number of ceilings left to paint is:\n\n$$\n28 - 15 = 13\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n$$\n\\boxed{13}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?", "answer": "After this week, Michelangelo will have 28-12=<<28-12=16>>16 more ceilings to paint.\nNext week, Michelangelo will paint 12/4=<<12/4=3>>3 ceilings.\nMichelangelo will have 16-3=<<16-3=13>>13 more ceilings to paint.\n#### 13", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "solutio