 me calculate that. Since each bag has 5 carrots, then the number of bags would be 365 divided by 5. Let me do the division. 365 divided by 5. Well, 5 times 70 is 350, so 365 would be 70 bags plus 15 more carrots. Wait, no, that's over. Wait, 5 times 70 is 350, so 5 times 72 is 360, which is 360. 365 - 360 is 5, so 72 bags plus 1 bag, so 73 bags? Wait, no, 72 bags would give 360 carrots, and 1 more bag is 5 carrots. So 72 + 1 = 73. So 73 bags?\n\nWait, let me check with another method. 365 divided by 5. Let me write it out:\n\n5 × 70 = 350, 5 × 71 = 355, 5 × 72 = 360, 5 × 73 = 365. Yes, exactly. So 73 bags.\n\nTherefore, 73 bags at $2 per bag would cost 73 × 2. Let me compute that. 70×2=140, 3×2=6, so total is 140 + 6 = $146.\n\nWait, so is the answer $146? Let me double-check.\n\nFirst, number of carrots per year: 365. Number of bags: 365 / 5 = 73. Total cost: 73 × $2 = $146. That seems correct.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's any chance I made a mistake with the number of days. The problem says \"in one year\". If it's over a year, then 365 days. That seems right. So 1 carrot per day. Yep, so 365.\n\nAnother way to think: each day is 1/, So in a year, he feeds for 365 days. So 365 carrots. Each bag is 5, so 365 divided by 5 is 73. So 73 bags × $2. So yeah, $146. Seems right.\n\nBut let me check once more. Suppose he feeds 1 carrot per day. So per day, $0. So over a year, he gets 365 carrots. Each bag is 1 kg (but here it's 1 port, so same). To find how many bags, 365 divided by 5. Yep, 73 bags. Total cost $2/each. So 73×2=146.\n\nI think that's correct. So the answer is $146. So written in a box, \\boxed{146}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by understanding the information given:\n\n- Harris feeds his dog **1 large organic carrot per day**.\n- There are **5 carrots in a 1 pound bag**, and each bag costs **$2.00**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the number of carrots per year\nThere are **365 days in a year**. Since Harris feeds his dog 1 carrot each day, the total number of carrots he will consume in a year is:\n\n$$\n365 \\text{ carrots/day} \\times 1 \\text{ day/year} = 365 \\text{ carrots}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of bags required\nEach bag contains **5 carrots**, so to find the number of bags:\n\n$$\n\\frac{365 \\text{ carrots}}{5 \\text{ carrots per bag}} = 73 \\text{ bags}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the total cost\nSince each bag costs **$2.00**, the total cost for 73 bags is:\n\n$$\n73 \\times 2 = 146\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{146}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Harris feeds his dog 1 large organic carrot over the course of 1 day.  There are 5 carrots in a 1 pound bag and each bag costs $2.00.  In one year, how much will Harris spend on carrots?", "answer": "His dog gets 1 carrot a day and there are 365 days in a year, so his dog eats 1*365 = <<1*365=365>>365 carrots\nThere are 5 carrots per bag and he will need 365 carrots, so that's 365/5 = 73 bags of carrots\nThe bags cost $2.00 and he will need 73 bags so that's $2*73 = $<<2*73=146.00>>146.00 worth of organic carrots\n#### 146", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Harris feeds his dog 1 large organic carrot over the course of 1 day.  There are 5 carrots in a 1 pound bag and each bag costs $2.00.  In one year, how much will Harris spend on carrots?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["146"], "solution": "146"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 21071, 34396, 806, 5562, 220, 16, 3460, 17356, 74194, 916, 279, 3308, 315, 220, 16, 1899, 13, 220, 2619, 525, 220, 20, 61417, 304, 264, 220, 16, 30023, 8968, 323, 1817, 8968, 7049, 400, 17, 13, 15], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 21071, 34396, 806, 5562, 220, 16, 3460, 17356, 74194, 817, 1