2026-10-30 04:10:24 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 22, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Sabrina gave 10 cookies to her brother. Her mother gave Sabrina half the number of cookies she gave her brother. Then Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. If Sabrina had 20 cookies at the start, how many cookies are left with her?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Sabrina gave 10 cookies to her brother. Her mother gave Sabrina half the number of cookies she gave her brother. Then Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. If Sabrina had 20 cookies at the start, how many cookies are left with her?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out this problem. So, Sabrina did some things with cookies, and we need to find out how many are left with her. Let me break it down step by step. \n\nFirst, the problem says Sabrina gave 10 cookies to her brother. Then her mother gave her half of what she gave her brother. Then Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. Also, initially, Sabrina had 20 cookies. We need to find out how many are left.\n\nAlright, let's start with the initial number of cookies. She had 20. Then she gave 10 to her brother. So that would mean she had 20 - 10 = 10 cookies left after giving 10 to her brother. \n\nWait, but then her mother gave her half of what she gave her brother. Remember, she gave her brother 10 cookies. So half of that would be 5 cookies. So her mother gave her 5 more cookies. That means she had 10 (from the first part) + 5 (from the second part) = 15 cookies. Let me check that again. Yes, brother got 10, mother gave her 5, so total so far is 15. \n\nThen Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. She had 15, so two-thirds of that would be (2/3)*15. Let me compute that. 15 divided by 3 is 5, so 5 times 2 is 10. So two-thirds of her cookies to her sister is 10. \n\nSo now, she had 15 (after mother gave her her half) minus 10 (she gave to her sister) equals 5 cookies. Therefore, the answer should be 5 left. \n\nBut let me double-check each part to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let me go through each step again.\n\nStart with initial cookies: 20.\n\nFirst act: gave 10 to brother. 20 - 10 = 10 left.\n\nMother gave her half of what she gave brother. She gave brother 10 cookies, so half is 5. So mother added 5 to her total. So 10 + 5 = 15.\n\nSecond act: gave two-thirds of her cookies to sister. She had 15. Two-thirds of 15 is indeed 10. So subtract that from 15: 15 - 10 = 5. \n\nYes, that seems to check out. So the answer should be 5. \n\nI don't see any errors in my reasoning. All steps are straightforward, and each time I checked a step to ensure that I had the correct value. So I think that's it. The answer is 5 cookies left.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{5}\n</think>\n\nTo solve this problem, we start with Sabrina's initial number of cookies, which is stated as 20.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Give 10 cookies to her brother\nSabrina gives 10 cookies to her brother.  \nThis results in:\n$$ 20 - 10 = 10 \\text{ cookies left} $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Receive half of the number of cookies from her mother\nHer mother gives Sabrina half of what she gave her brother. Since she gave 10 cookies to her brother, half is:\n$$ \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ cookies} $$\n\nSo, her total cookies after receiving the 5 cookies becomes:\n$$ 10 + 5 = 15 \\text{ cookies} $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Give two-thirds of her cookies to her sister\nNow, Sabrina has 15 cookies and gives two-thirds of them to her sister. The value of two-thirds of 15 is:\n$$ \\frac{2}{3} \\times 15 = 10 \\text{ cookies} $$\n\nSo, the cookies remaining after giving 10 to her sister are:\n$$ 15 - 10 = 5 \\text{ cookies} $$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$ \\boxed{5} $$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Sabrina gave 10 cookies to her brother. Her mother gave Sabrina half the number of cookies she gave her brother. Then Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. If Sabrina had 20 cookies at the start, how many cookies are left with her?", "answer": "Sabrina had 20 - 10 = <<20-10=10>>10 cookies left after giving 10 cookies to her brother.\nSabrina's mother gave her 10/2 = <<10/2=5>>5 cookies.\nSo, she had 10 + 5 = <<10+5=15>>15 cookies then.\nShe gave 15 x 2/3 = <<15*2/3=10>>10 cookies to her sister.\nTherefore, she only has 15 - 10 = <<15-10=5>>5 cookies left.\n#### 5", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Sabrina gave 10 cookies to her brother. Her mother gave Sabrina half the number of cookies she gave her brother. Then Sabrina gave two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. If Sabrina had 20 cookies at the start, how many cookies are left with her?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["5"], "solution": "5"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 20603, 39971, 6551, 220, 16, 15, 8279, 311, 1059, 10641, 13, 6252, 6554, 6551, 20603, 39971, 4279, 279, 1372, 315, 8279, 1340, 6551, 1059, 10641, 13, 5005, 20603, 39971, 6551, 1378, 44626, 315, 1059, 8279, 311, 1059], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 13, 2055, 11, 20603, 39971, 1521, 1045, 2513, 448, 8279, 11, 323, 582, 1184, 311, 1477, 700, 1246, 1657, 525, 2115, 448, 1059, 13, 6771, 752, 1438, 432, 1495, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 4710, 5338, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 20603, 39971, 6551, 220, 16, 15, 8279, 311, 1059, 10641, 13, 5005, 1059]}
