s, which is 1000 divided by 10, that's 100. But since he can't buy partial tanks, he needs 20 tanks. Yeah, that matches. \n\nSo the answer should be 20 tanks. Let me just verify once more. Let me do the math again step by step.\n\nTotal required liters: 1000 balloons * 10 liters/balloon = 10,000 liters.\n\nNumber of tanks needed: 10,000 liters / 500 liters/tank = 20 tanks.\n\nYep, that's correct. So the answer is 20.\n</think>\n\nJohn buys 1000 balloons, and each balloon holds 10 liters of air. To compute the total amount of air required for the balloons, we multiply the number of balloons by the volume per balloon:\n\n$$\n1000 \\text{ balloons} \\times 10 \\text{ liters/balloon} = 10,000 \\text{ liters}\n$$\n\nHe needs to fill the balloons using 500-liter tanks. To determine how many tanks are needed, we divide the total liters by the capacity of each tank:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,000 \\text{ liters}}{500 \\text{ liters/tank}} = 20 \\text{ tanks}\n$$\n\nThus, the number of tanks John needs to buy is $\\boxed{20}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 