 5 times 20 is 100. Then total students = 300 + 100 = 400. So, 400 students now.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of it as (15 + 5) classes, each with 20 students. So 20 classes * 20 students = 400. Yeah, same answer.\n\nLet me check again. Original classes: 15 * 20 = 300. Added classes: 5 * 20 = 100. Total students = 300 + 100 = 400. Yep, seems right.\n\nI don't think there's any trick here. It's straightforward. So the answer should be 400. Let me just confirm once more. 15 classes, 20 each: 15*20 is 300. 5 added classes, 20 each: 5*20 is 100. 300+100=400. Yep, that's correct.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem:\n\n1. **Initial number of classes and students**: \n   - Texas Integrated School has **15 classes**.\n   - Each class has **20 students**.\n   - Total students initially:  \n     $ 15 \\times 20 = 300 $ students.\n\n2. **Adding five more classes**:\n   - Five new classes will each have **20 students**.\n   - Total students added:  \n     $ 5 \\times 20 = 100 $ students.\n\n3. **Total number of students now**:  \n   $ 300 + 100 = \\boxed{400} $.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?", "answer": "The school has 15 x 20 = <<15*20=300>>300 students right now.\nThey have an additional 5 x 20 = <<5*20=100>>100 students after adding 5 more classes.\nTherefore, Texas Integrated School will have a total of 300+100= <<300+100=400>>400 students.\n#### 400", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["400"], "solution": "400"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6