2026-10-30 04:37:17 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 42, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out how much more Jame makes per year in his new job compared to his old job. Hmm, so first, I need to compare his annual earnings from both jobs and find the difference. \n\nFirst, let me write down the information I have:\n\n- Jame's new job: $20 per hour, 40 hours a week.\n- His old job: $16 per hour, 25 hours a week.\n\nHe works 52 weeks a year, so I need to calculate his new job's annual earnings and the old job's annual earnings, then subtract the old ones from the new ones to find the difference.\n\nLet me start with the new job. \n\nFor the new job:\nHours per week: 40\nHour rate: $20\n\nSo, per week earnings for the new job: 40 * 20.\n\nLet me calculate that. 40 * 20 is 800. So, 800 dollars per week for the new job.\n\nThen, for the old job:\nHours per week: 25\nHour rate: $16\n\nSo, per week earnings for the old job: 25 * 16.\n\nLet me do that calculation. 25 * 16 is 400. So, 400 dollars per week for the old job.\n\nNow, to find the difference per week, subtract the old job earnings from the new one: 800 - 400 = 400 dollars per week.\n\nNow, since he works 52 weeks a year, multiply the weekly difference by 52 to get the total annual difference.\n\nSo, total annual difference: 400 * 52.\n\nLet me compute that. 400 * 52... Hmm, 400*50 = 20,000 and 400*2 = 800, so total would be 20,800.\n\nSo, Jame makes $20,800 more per year in his new job compared to his old job.\n\nWait, let me check my calculations again to make sure I didn't make any errors.\n\nNew job weeks: 40, rate $20. 40*20 = 800. Old job: 25*16 = 400. Difference per week is 400. Multiply by 52: 400*52. Yes, that seems correct.\n\nBut let me do the multiplication again: 400*50 is 20,000 and 400*2 is 800, so total 20,800. Yeah, that's right.\n\nSo, the answer should be $20,800.\n\nBut let me think if there's another way to approach this to verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe calculate the percentage increase first. But since we need exactly money, not a percentage, it's probably better to stick with multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, compute total years. But no, he works 52 weeks per year, so that part is covered.\n\nWait, another check: if he works 52 weeks, so 52*40 hours = 2,080 hours per year. Old job: 52*25 = 1,300 hours per year. So, new hours paid per year: 2,080. Old hours paid: 1,300. So, difference in hours is 2,080 - 1,300 = 780 hours. Then, difference in earnings would be 780 hours * 40 per hour (new rate) - same 780 * 16 (old rate) ?\n\nWait, no, wait. Wait, maybe that's complicating. Let me see:\n\nDifference in hours: (40*20) - (25*16) = 800 - 400 = 400 per week. Then total annual difference: 400 * 52 = 20,800. Same result. So, that seems correct.\n\nYes, so I think that's the right answer. The difference is $20,800.\n\n**Final Answer**\nJame makes \\boxed{20800} dollars more per year in his new job than the old job.\n</think>\n\nTo find out how much more Jame makes per year in his new job compared to his old job, we start by computing the earnings of both jobs over the course of a year.\n\n---\n\n### **New Job Earnings**\n\n- Hourly rate: $20\n- Hours per week: 40\n- Weeks per year: 52\n\n**Weekly earnings for new job:**\n$$\n40 \\text{ hours/week} \\times 20 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 800 \\text{ dollars/week}\n$$\n\n**Annual earnings for the new job:**\n$$\n800 \\text{ dollars/week} \\times 52 \\text{ weeks/year} = 41,600 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Old Job Earnings**\n\n- Hourly rate: $16\n- Hours per week: 25\n- Weeks per year: 52\n\n**Weekly earnings for old job:**\n$$\n25 \\text{ hours/week} \\times 16 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 400 \\text{ dollars/week}\n$$\n\n**Annual earnings for the old job:**\n$$\n400 \\text{ dollars/week} \\times 52 \\text{ weeks/year} = 20,800 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Difference in Annual Earnings**\n\n$$\n41,600 \\text{ dollars (new)} - 20,800 \\text{ dollars (old)} = \\boxed{20800}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n\nJame makes $\\boxed{20800}$ dollars more per year in his new job than the old job.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?", "answer": "He makes 20*40=$<<20*40=800>>800 per week\nHe used to make 16*25=$<<16*25=400>>400 per week\nSo his raise was 800-400=$<<800-400=400>>400 per week\nSo he makes 400*52=$<<400*52=20800>>20,800 per year more\n#### 20800", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20800"], "solution": "20800"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 1