ms had 45, American Summits definitely has 2 * 45, which is 90 bottled water. \n\nHormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated. So, 3 * 45 is 135 dressed chickens. \n\nBoudin Butchers donated one-third the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated. Hormel's dress chickens are 135, so Boudin Butchers donated 1/3 of that, which is 45. \n\nDel Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. American Summits had 90 bottled water. So Del Monte donated 90 - 30 = 60 bottles. \n\nNow, to find the total food items, I need to add up all the items donated by each company. Let me add them step by step. \n\nFoster Farms: 45 chickens\nAmerican Summits: 90 bottles of water\nHormel: 135 dressed chickens\nBoudin Butchers: 45 dressed chickens\nDel Monte: 60 bottles of water\n\nAdding these up: 45 (chickens) + 90 (water) + 135 (chickens) + 45 (chickens) + 60 (water). \n\nLet me compute that. Let me group the chickens: 45 + 135 + 45. 45 + 135 is 180, plus 45 is 225. So the chickens add up to 225. Then the water: 90 + 60. 90 + 60 is 150. Then total items is 225 + 150 = 375. \n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. \n\nFoster: 45\nAmerican: 2*45=90\nHormel: 3*45=135\nBoudin: 1/3 of Hormel's 135, so 45\nDel Monte: 90-30=60\n\nAdd them: 45 + 90 is 135, +135 is 270, +45 is 315, +60 is 375. Yeah, so total is 375.\n\nWait, but hold on, when I added 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 + 60, does that equal 375? Let me check another way. \n\nAdding all the chickens: 45 + 135 + 45. 45 + 135 is 180, plus 45 is 225. \n\nAdding all the water: 90 + 60. 90 + 60 is 150. 225 + 150 is 375. Yep. So I think that's correct. \n\nI don't see any errors in my calculations. All the numbers check out, and the logic follows. The answer is 375.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of food items donated by the companies is \\boxed{375}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the total number of food items donated by all the companies, we need to calculate the total number of items donated by each company and then add them together.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Breakdown\n\n1. **Foster Farms**:  \n   - Donated **45 dressed chickens**.\n\n2. **American Summits**:  \n   - Donated **twice** the number of bottled water than Foster Farms.  \n   - $ 2 \\times 45 = 90 $ bottles of water.\n\n3. **Hormel**:  \n   - Donated **three times** the number of dressed chickens than Foster Farms.  \n   - $ 3 \\times 45 = 135 $ dressed chickens.\n\n4. **Boudin Butchers**:  \n   - Donated **one-third** the number of dressed chickens than Hormel.  \n   - $ \\frac{1}{3} \\times 135 = 45 $ dressed chickens.\n\n5. **Del Monte Foods**:  \n   - Donated **30 fewer bottles of water** than American Summits.  \n   - $ 90 - 30 = 60 $ bottles of water.\n\n---\n\n### Total Items Donated\n\nSum up all the items donated:\n\n- **Chickens**: $45 + 135 + 45 = 225$\n- **Bottled water**: $90 + 60 = 150$\n\n$$\n\\text{Total food items} = 225 + 150 = \\boxed{375}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{375}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?", "answer": "American Summits donated 45 x 2 = <<45*2=90>>90 bottled waters.\nHormel donated 45 x 3 = <<45*3=135>>135 spams.\nBoudin Bakery donated 135 x 1/3 = <<135*1/3=45>>45 sourdoughs.\nDel Monte Foods donated 90 - 30 = <<90-30=60>>60 canned fruits.\nTherefore, a total of 45 + 90 + 135 + 45 + 60 = <<45+90+135+45+60=375>>375 different food.\n#### 375", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["375"], "solution": "375"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 20924, 3607, 5110, 29538, 264, 2205, 3607, 6073, 13, 38174, 67021, 33368, 220, 19, 20, 25365, 53523, 26, 3693, 8116, 44703, 33368, 10917, 279, 1372, 315, 71078, 3015, 1091, 279, 1372, 315, 25365, 16158, 33368, 553, 38174], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2619, 525, 4236, 3607, 5110, 60859, 3607, 3589, 13, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 279, 2790, 1372, 315, 3589, 807, 678, 33368, 13, 6771, 752, 1191, 553, 14838, 700, 279, 1995, 1817, 2813, 33368, 13, 4710, 5338, 11, 38174, 67021, 33368, 220, 19, 20, 25365, 53523, 13, 6771, 752, 5185, 429, 1495, 13, 4710, 5847, 11]}
